.TH ADDR2LINE 1 "March 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
addr2line \- convert addresses to file names and line numbers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B addr2line
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIADDRESS\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B addr2line
translates program addresses into source locations using DWARF debug data.
By default it reads from
.I a.out
and prints one
.IR file:line
result per query.
.PP
If no addresses are provided on the command line, addresses are read from
standard input, one per line.
.SH OPTIONS
As with
.BR getopt_long (3),
options and addresses may come in any order
.RB ( "\-e a.out 0x401000 \-f" ):
every argument that is not an option is an address.
.B \-\-
ends the options, and so does the first address when
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set; a lone
.B \-
is an address.
Short options may be bundled
.RB ( "\-fpCe a.out" ).
The value of
.BR \-b ,
.B \-e
or
.B \-j
may follow the letter directly
.RB ( \-ea.out ", " \-j.text )
or be the next argument.
A long option's value may follow an
.B =
or be the next argument
.RB ( "\-\-exe a.out" ),
and a long option may be abbreviated to any prefix that names it
alone
.RB ( \-\-func ),
or that names only spellings of the same option
.RB ( \-\-rec ).
.TP
.BI \-b " TARGET\fR,\fP " \-\-target= TARGET
Require the input to be in the format GNU
.B addr2line
calls
.IR TARGET :
.BR elf64\-x86\-64 ,
.BR elf32\-x86\-64 ,
.BR elf32\-i386 ,
.BR elf64\-little ,
.BR elf64\-big ,
.BR elf32\-little ,
.BR elf32\-big ,
or
.BR default ,
which takes any file.
A file in another format is an error (\(lqfile format not
recognized\(rq), as is any other name (\(lqinvalid bfd target\(rq).
.TP
.BI \-e " FILE\fR,\fP " \-\-exe= FILE
Use
.I FILE
as the input ELF object (default:
.IR a.out ).
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-functions
Print function names before source locations.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-inlines
When the address lies in inlined code, also print the functions it was
inlined into, from innermost to outermost.
The innermost function is shown with the line the address belongs to;
each outer function is shown with the line of the call that was inlined
into it, ending with the function that contains the code.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-basenames
Strip directory prefixes from file names.
.TP
.BI \-j " SECTION\fR,\fP " \-\-section= SECTION
Treat input addresses as offsets in
.IR SECTION .
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-demangle\fR[=\fISTYLE\fR]
Demangle function names using
.BR demangle (3).
.I STYLE
is one of
.B auto
(the default: the scheme is told from the name),
.B gnu\-v3
or
.B java
(Itanium C++),
.BR rust ,
.BR dlang ,
.BR none ,
which leaves names as they are, or
.BR gnat ,
which is accepted but, there being no Ada demangler, also leaves names
as they are.
Any other style is an error.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-no\-recurse\-limit ", " \-\-no\-recursion\-limit
Let
.B \-C
demangle names nested however deeply
.RB ( DEMANGLE_NO_RECURSE_LIMIT
in
.BR demangle (3)).
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recurse\-limit ", " \-\-recursion\-limit
Keep the demangler's bound on nesting (the default); undoes an
earlier
.BR \-r .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty\-print
Print each frame on one line: with
.B \-f
as
.IR "function at file:line" ,
and without it as just
.IR file:line .
With
.BR \-i ,
each frame after the first begins with
.RB \(lq " (inlined by) " \(rq.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-addresses
Print the queried address once before its result: on a line of its own,
or with
.B \-p
as an
.RB \(lq 0x \fIaddr\fP: \(rq
prefix on the first frame.
.TP
.B \-c
Print
.IR file:line:column
when column data is available.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-H ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit with status 0.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-v ", " \-\-version
Print version information and exit with status 0.
.SH OUTPUT
If a lookup fails,
.B addr2line
prints:
.PP
.RS
??
.br
??:0
.RE
.PP
with
.B \-f
enabled, or just:
.PP
.RS
??:0
.RE
.PP
without
.BR \-f .
With
.B \-p
the frame is
.RB \(lq "?? ??:0" \(rq
with
.B \-f
and
.RB \(lq ??:0 \(rq
without it; there is no
.RB \(lq " at " \(rq,
as in GNU
.BR addr2line .
.PP
As in GNU
.BR addr2line ,
no address is rejected.
Text that starts with a digit, or with a hex letter and has no
.BR + ,
is read as
.BR strtoull (3)
reads a hexadecimal number: an optional sign and
.BR 0x ,
then the leading hex digits, so
.B 10zz
is 0x10.
Anything else is
.IR symbol [\fB+\fP offset ]:
the value of that symbol, found by its name or its demangled name,
plus
.I offset
(in C notation), or 0 when there is no such symbol, so
.BR zz ,
.B \-5
and
.B \-
are all 0.
An ELF32 address is cut to 32 bits.
.SH ADDRESS ADJUSTMENT
For
.B ET_DYN
objects (PIE and shared libraries),
.B addr2line
applies load-bias style correction when absolute runtime addresses are given.
.SH DWARF SUPPORT
Supported DWARF versions: v2, v3, v4, v5.
.PP
Function names come from the
.B DW_TAG_subprogram
that covers the address, or, when the address lies in inlined code, the
innermost
.B DW_TAG_inlined_subroutine
that covers it.
A subprogram with no name of its own, such as an out-of-line C++ member
function definition or an out-of-line copy of an inline function, is
named through its
.B DW_AT_specification
or
.B DW_AT_abstract_origin
reference.
The ELF symbol table is used only when no subprogram names the address.
.PP
File names and line numbers come from the
.I .debug_line
sequence that covers the address.
A sequence ends at its
.B DW_LNE_end_sequence
row; one whose line program stops without that row ends at the address
of its last row, which therefore resolves to nothing, as do addresses
beyond it.
Where sequences overlap, the one that starts first wins, then the longer
one.
.PP
Supported compressed debug formats:
.I .zdebug_*
(GNU zlib)
and
.I SHF_COMPRESSED
debug sections.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including unresolved addresses).
.TP
.B 1
Fatal error, such as invalid ELF format, unreadable input file, or bad options.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Resolve two addresses from an executable:
.PP
.RS
.EX
addr2line -e ./kernel.elf 0x8049123 0x8049567
.EE
.RE
.TP
Resolve runtime-style PIE addresses with function names:
.PP
.RS
.EX
addr2line -f -e ./app.pie 0x555551a3 0x5555520f
.EE
.RE
.TP
Show the inline chain of an address in one-line form:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ addr2line -f -i -p -a -s -e ./t 0x401144
0x401144: inner at t.c:2
 (inlined by) mid at t.c:4
 (inlined by) outer at t.c:5
.EE
.RE
.TP
Read addresses from standard input:
.PP
.RS
.EX
printf '0x401050\\n0x401080\\n' | addr2line -e ./a.out
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR demangle (3),
.BR c++filt (1),
.BR nm (1),
.BR objdump (1),
.BR readelf (1)
